1
00:00:07,799 --> 00:00:12,960
The narrator is going to describe the worst thing
in this account, and is going to do

2
00:00:12,980 --> 00:00:17,410
it without describing it, and asks you to
accept that this is deliberate.

3
00:00:17,809 --> 00:00:23,883
In the winter of 2043 Dr. Evelyn Marsh's group,
working on the heritable lineage, produced

4
00:00:23,903 --> 00:00:29,773
a construct that did not do what she wanted. It did
something else. She spent four days

5
00:00:29,793 --> 00:00:35,799
characterising it and then she did a thing that she
had not done in fourteen years, which

6
00:00:35,819 --> 00:00:41,622
was to seal a programme, lock the data, and request
the Director in person rather than

7
00:00:41,642 --> 00:00:42,570
send a report.

8
00:00:42,969 --> 00:00:46,859
He came in on the second of February.
She met him on the ice.

9
00:00:47,380 --> 00:00:52,069
I'm going to tell you what it does and I'm not going
to tell you how it works, and then

10
00:00:52,089 --> 00:00:56,128
I'm going to ask you to destroy it, and I want
that sequence on the record.

11
00:00:56,529 --> 00:00:57,359
Go on.

12
00:00:57,759 --> 00:01:02,921
It is airborne. It is quiet for nineteen days. It
has no treatment, because the thing

13
00:01:02,941 --> 00:01:08,104
it breaks is the thing every treatment works
through. And it does not kill everybody,

14
00:01:08,124 --> 00:01:13,409
which is the part that I have not slept about — it
kills approximately one in nine, and

15
00:01:13,429 --> 00:01:17,800
the other eight are fine, and they carry it
for the rest of their lives.

16
00:01:18,629 --> 00:01:23,781
A weapon that kills everyone is a suicide note. A
weapon that kills one in nine is a *policy

17
00:01:23,801 --> 00:01:28,670
instrument*. Somebody would use this. Somebody would
genuinely use this, because they'd

18
00:01:28,690 --> 00:01:33,673
tell themselves eight out of nine is survivable, and
they'd be right, and that's why it's

19
00:01:33,693 --> 00:01:35,753
the worst thing anyone has ever made.

20
00:01:36,153 --> 00:01:42,073
The narrator notes that Dr. Evelyn Marsh, who had by
then done things in fourteen levels

21
00:01:42,093 --> 00:01:47,812
of ice that no ethics board in the world would have
permitted, and who had never once

22
00:01:47,832 --> 00:01:53,483
flinched, was crying while she said this, and that
it is the only recorded occasion.

23
00:01:54,003 --> 00:01:55,673
How many people know?

24
00:01:56,072 --> 00:01:59,862
Four. Me, two of my seconds, both
Absolute, and now you.

25
00:02:00,263 --> 00:02:01,493
And the material?

26
00:02:01,893 --> 00:02:07,300
Eleven vials, in the vault under my feet, and one
complete description that exists on

27
00:02:07,320 --> 00:02:11,323
one air-gapped array that has never been
connected to anything.

28
00:02:12,163 --> 00:02:16,552
(very cold) Dr. Marsh, I am not
going to destroy it.

29
00:02:16,953 --> 00:02:22,596
She had known that for four days. She had rehearsed
nine arguments on the ice. She used

30
00:02:22,616 --> 00:02:23,443
none of them.

31
00:02:23,962 --> 00:02:27,483
(very soft) Say why. Properly. Not
the clever version.

32
00:02:27,883 --> 00:02:34,923
Because I can now do something that no one has been
able to do since 1945, which is to

33
00:02:34,943 --> 00:02:42,393
make the use of the worst thing in the world
*unprofitable*. Not impossible — unprofitable.

34
00:02:42,413 --> 00:02:49,617
There are twenty-three state programmes on this
planet working towards something in this

35
00:02:49,637 --> 00:02:56,594
class, and four of them are closer than anyone
believes. In nine years, somebody will

36
00:02:56,614 --> 00:03:01,273
have it who does not have your
conscience or my patience.

37
00:03:01,554 --> 00:03:07,751
And on that day, the only thing that will stop them
is the certainty that somebody else

38
00:03:07,771 --> 00:03:11,323
already has something worse and
has never used it.

39
00:03:11,723 --> 00:03:14,124
That's the argument every one
of them makes.

40
00:03:14,525 --> 00:03:21,545
(very cold) Yes. And it has worked for ninety-nine
years, and I have read every alternative,

41
00:03:21,565 --> 00:03:27,054
and there is nothing else in the literature
that has ever worked at all.

42
00:03:27,455 --> 00:03:33,738
There is a second half to the conversation on the ice
and it has never been quoted, because

43
00:03:33,758 --> 00:03:39,764
Evelyn Marsh cut it out of the transcript she filed
and restored it in 2091 in a sealed

44
00:03:39,784 --> 00:03:42,605
addendum that was opened after her death.

45
00:03:43,125 --> 00:03:46,211
Ask me the question you actually
came here to ask.

46
00:03:46,611 --> 00:03:48,061
I have asked it.

47
00:03:48,461 --> 00:03:52,931
You asked me how many people know. You have not
asked me whether I can make more.

48
00:03:53,882 --> 00:03:55,711
(very cold) Can you?

49
00:03:56,111 --> 00:04:01,188
In about four days, from the sealed material, with
two of my people. In about nine months

50
00:04:01,208 --> 00:04:06,228
from scratch if the vault were destroyed tonight and
you shot me in the morning, because

51
00:04:06,248 --> 00:04:11,096
the route is not difficult and the route is the
thing that frightens me, and it is in

52
00:04:11,116 --> 00:04:15,963
my head and it will be in somebody else's head
within twenty years whatever either of

53
00:04:15,983 --> 00:04:16,422
us does.

54
00:04:16,701 --> 00:04:21,884
(very slow) That is what I actually came to tell you.
Not that I made something terrible.

55
00:04:21,904 --> 00:04:27,379
That it was *easy*, and that the only reason no one else
has is that nobody with the equipment

56
00:04:27,399 --> 00:04:28,432
has wanted to yet.

57
00:04:28,831 --> 00:04:34,672
The narrator notes that this is the most frightening
sentence in this account, that it

58
00:04:34,692 --> 00:04:40,668
was spoken by a woman standing on an ice shelf in
2044, and that it was the entire basis

59
00:04:40,688 --> 00:04:46,801
of the Brussels Register: not that Umbrella had a
weapon, but that Evelyn Marsh had proved

60
00:04:46,821 --> 00:04:52,662
a weapon was four days of work for anybody with a
good laboratory and a bad afternoon.

61
00:04:53,182 --> 00:04:58,312
Then the argument I am about to make to
nineteen generals is true.

62
00:04:58,711 --> 00:05:03,948
(very soft) The argument you are about to make to
nineteen generals is true and I am the

63
00:05:03,968 --> 00:05:09,204
reason it is true, and I would like you to remember,
when they applaud you for it, which

64
00:05:09,224 --> 00:05:10,877
of us has to live with that.

65
00:05:11,278 --> 00:05:13,748
The second half of this chapter is a room.

66
00:05:14,148 --> 00:05:20,695
In September of 2044, at the request of four
governments who had been asking for six months,

67
00:05:20,715 --> 00:05:27,191
Albert Wesker attended a closed session of a NATO
planning committee, in a building without

68
00:05:27,211 --> 00:05:33,616
windows, with nineteen people in it: four chiefs of
defence, three intelligence directors,

69
00:05:33,636 --> 00:05:39,969
two foreign ministers, and a Deputy Secretary General
who spent the entire ninety minutes

70
00:05:39,989 --> 00:05:41,112
taking no notes.

71
00:05:41,512 --> 00:05:47,302
They had come to ask him about the Security Service,
about the Tyrants, and about a rumour.

72
00:05:47,701 --> 00:05:52,828
He answered all three questions in eleven minutes,
and the narrator will give you the

73
00:05:52,848 --> 00:05:53,192
third.

74
00:05:53,711 --> 00:05:59,105
There is a persistent report, from four separate
services, that Umbrella has developed

75
00:05:59,125 --> 00:06:04,644
a biological agent of strategic class. I am going to
ask you directly, in this room, and

76
00:06:04,664 --> 00:06:06,722
I would ask you not to insult us.

77
00:06:07,672 --> 00:06:08,542
Yes.

78
00:06:08,942 --> 00:06:14,223
There is a recording. The Deputy Secretary General
can be heard putting down a glass.

79
00:06:14,742 --> 00:06:16,132
You're confirming it.

80
00:06:16,531 --> 00:06:23,503
I am confirming that it exists, that it is held at a
facility I will not name, that four

81
00:06:23,523 --> 00:06:30,415
people know its nature, that it has never been
weaponised in any deliverable sense, and

82
00:06:30,435 --> 00:06:32,401
that I will never use it.

83
00:06:32,802 --> 00:06:34,372
Then why have it?

84
00:06:34,771 --> 00:06:37,122
Because you are going to build one.

85
00:06:37,786 --> 00:06:44,279
Not you personally, Minister. Not this alliance,
this decade. But there are seventeen

86
00:06:44,299 --> 00:06:50,944
programmes in this class on this planet right now,
and I can name nine of them, and two

87
00:06:50,964 --> 00:06:57,457
of them are in states represented at this table. In
about nine years one of them will

88
00:06:57,477 --> 00:07:03,969
succeed, and the people who succeed will be a
committee, and a committee has never in

89
00:07:03,989 --> 00:07:10,634
the history of this species declined to use a thing
it has paid for once it has stopped

90
00:07:10,654 --> 00:07:12,397
being frightened of it.

91
00:07:12,677 --> 00:07:19,486
(very cold) So I have made mine first, and I have
told you I have it, and I have told

92
00:07:19,506 --> 00:07:26,476
you I will not use it. And from this afternoon,
every one of those seventeen programmes

93
00:07:26,496 --> 00:07:33,547
has a new problem, which is that the most capable
organisation on earth is already ahead

94
00:07:33,567 --> 00:07:38,047
of them and has publicly forsworn the
use of the result.

95
00:07:38,447 --> 00:07:40,007
A silence of some length.

96
00:07:40,526 --> 00:07:42,896
And what stops you changing your mind?

97
00:07:43,297 --> 00:07:45,147
Nothing whatsoever.

98
00:07:45,547 --> 00:07:51,296
The narrator notes that he said it without any emphasis
at all, as a man states the weather,

99
00:07:51,316 --> 00:07:57,065
and that four of the nineteen people in that room later
described it as the most frightening

100
00:07:57,085 --> 00:07:58,571
moment of their careers.

101
00:07:59,091 --> 00:08:07,356
Gentlemen, I want you to understand precisely what I am
offering, because it is not reassurance.

102
00:08:07,376 --> 00:08:14,952
I am proposing a standing arrangement. Umbrella will
publish, annually, a full inventory

103
00:08:14,972 --> 00:08:22,288
of every strategic-class biological programme on
this planet, including its own, with

104
00:08:22,308 --> 00:08:29,711
locations, staffing and progress, to every
government represented at this table and to

105
00:08:29,731 --> 00:08:32,301
the World Health Organization.

106
00:08:32,701 --> 00:08:34,671
You cannot possibly know —

107
00:08:35,071 --> 00:08:37,600
Minister, I know about yours.

108
00:08:38,321 --> 00:08:44,899
I have known for four years. It is at a site nine
kilometres from a town that I will not

109
00:08:44,919 --> 00:08:51,797
name in front of this many people, it employs forty-one
scientists, and its second programme

110
00:08:51,817 --> 00:08:58,470
was suspended in March for reasons that your own
health ministry recorded as an equipment

111
00:08:58,490 --> 00:09:04,843
fault. I will send you the file tonight and you
will find it is correct, and then you

112
00:09:04,863 --> 00:09:11,216
will decide whether you would rather have me
publishing everybody's inventory or only

113
00:09:11,236 --> 00:09:12,190
your rivals'.

114
00:09:12,591 --> 00:09:19,144
The arrangement was signed by nine countries in 2045
and by thirty-one by 2049, and it

115
00:09:19,164 --> 00:09:25,718
is still in force. It is called the Brussels
Register, and Albert Wesker's name is not

116
00:09:25,738 --> 00:09:32,215
on it anywhere, and it did more to slow biological
proliferation than every treaty of

117
00:09:32,235 --> 00:09:33,821
the previous century.

118
00:09:34,221 --> 00:09:39,661
It was also, the narrator notes, the greatest
intelligence-collection instrument ever

119
00:09:39,681 --> 00:09:44,350
constructed, and every one of its signatories
knew that when they signed.

120
00:09:45,500 --> 00:09:53,675
Deterrence established. Sixteen strategic biological
programmes identified, nine suspended

121
00:09:53,695 --> 00:10:02,052
within four years. Military action against Umbrella by
any state: assessed as inconceivable.

122
00:10:02,072 --> 00:10:09,791
Forty million Points. Note: you have not built a
delivery system. I have checked. You

123
00:10:09,811 --> 00:10:11,794
have built a sentence.

124
00:10:12,385 --> 00:10:15,556
The voice in his skull chose that
moment to arrive.

125
00:10:16,076 --> 00:10:21,548
You told a room of generals that nothing
whatsoever stops you.

126
00:10:21,947 --> 00:10:22,837
Yes.

127
00:10:23,238 --> 00:10:31,189
That was not calculation. I have watched you
calculate for eighteen years and that was

128
00:10:31,209 --> 00:10:31,838
not it.

129
00:10:32,238 --> 00:10:38,759
(very cold) It was entirely calculation. A
reassurance would have been tested. A man who

130
00:10:38,779 --> 00:10:45,225
says *I would never* is a man who has thought about
it and made a promise, and promises

131
00:10:45,245 --> 00:10:52,064
are inspected. A man who says *nothing stops me* has
told them there is no point inspecting,

132
00:10:52,084 --> 00:10:58,308
and that the only variable is his character, and
that they had better hope it holds.

133
00:10:59,038 --> 00:11:00,503
And does it?

134
00:11:00,902 --> 00:11:02,812
The car went west through the rain.

135
00:11:03,331 --> 00:11:11,118
In eighteen years I have not killed a human being in
this world. Not one. Not Tolliver,

136
00:11:11,138 --> 00:11:18,745
not Carrick, not Crane, not the four men who came
over the wall in 2027. Every single

137
00:11:18,765 --> 00:11:26,461
person who has ever stood in front of me is alive,
and most of them are on my payroll.

138
00:11:26,861 --> 00:11:29,525
That is not an answer either.

139
00:11:29,927 --> 00:11:36,558
(very cold) No. It is a *record*, and a record is
the only thing anyone can be judged

140
00:11:36,578 --> 00:11:41,096
by, and mine is eighteen years long
and has nothing in it.

141
00:11:41,496 --> 00:11:47,563
The narrator will simply note the date on which
that ceases to be true, because it is

142
00:11:47,583 --> 00:11:53,649
coming and because Albert Wesker does not see it
coming: the ninth of June, 2047. Two

143
00:11:53,669 --> 00:11:56,226
years and nine months from that car.
